00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:30.710
Do you ever look at a microbe doing something
and think, “I wish I could do that?”

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:32.160
I don’t know…maybe you don’t.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.450
Maybe you don’t watch a vorticella casually
grab food from a distance and think, “Wow,

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:40.579
I wish I didn’t have to get up to get a
snack.”

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:45.930
And maybe you don’t watch a single-celled
eukaryote enter a protective cyst and think,

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:51.180
“Huh, wouldn’t it be neat if I could just
chill out in a bubble of my own making when

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:52.630
things get tough?”

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:58.469
And that’s fine, it’s good to be content
with a human existence.

00:00:58.469 --> 00:01:03.920
It’s got its perks: we get to think about
things and talk about things and make things.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:10.350
And for millennia, we’ve turned all of our
thoughts and conversations and creations into

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:15.670
culture and societies, which have helped us
better understand the world around us—whether

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:17.639
through art or science.

00:01:17.639 --> 00:01:22.810
We’ve learned about ourselves, about the
stars above us, the rocks below us, and everything

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:25.350
in between and beyond.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.270
And that includes microbes.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:31.359
We may not have known about microbes until
a few centuries ago, but that doesn’t mean

00:01:31.359 --> 00:01:35.979
we haven’t made use of them before—whether
that’s the yeast we used to make beer or

00:01:35.979 --> 00:01:38.619
the lactobacillus we used to ferment milk.

00:01:38.619 --> 00:01:42.189
But apparently, there is more to life than
food.

00:01:42.189 --> 00:01:47.219
And knowing that microbes exist has only led
to more questions about how they exist, which

00:01:47.219 --> 00:01:51.590
has only led to more inspiration for how we
can exist.

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:55.851
Counting up all the ways that scientists and
engineers have been looking to the microcosmos

00:01:55.851 --> 00:01:59.499
for inspiration would be an endless task.

00:01:59.499 --> 00:02:05.729
So today we’re going to focus on one idea
in particular: algae, and the possibility

00:02:05.729 --> 00:02:13.719
that one day, algae will fuel our cars, our
airplanes, even our spaceships.

00:02:13.719 --> 00:02:20.360
“Algae” is a pretty broad category that
roughly groups together all aquatic, photosynthetic

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:26.349
organisms, whether they are tiny cyanobacteria
or a large piece of kelp.

00:02:26.349 --> 00:02:33.019
But the algae we are most interested in today
are single-celled eukaryotes, like Botryococcus braunii.

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:38.610
If you want to find a living Botryococcus
braunii, you can look in fresh and brackish waters.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:45.269
And if you want to find fossilized Botryococcus,
you can dig through Precambrian oil shales,

00:02:45.269 --> 00:02:51.780
where their remains point to another more
remarkable fact: these tiny little algae are

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:57.099
the largest biological contributor to crude
oil in those shales.

00:02:57.099 --> 00:03:04.969
Now, it helps that when they’re alive, somewhere
between 25-75% of Botryococcus’ dry weight

00:03:04.969 --> 00:03:07.319
can be made up of oils.

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:13.870
So scientists have studied Botryococcus to
see if we can use the micro algae to make

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:21.239
those oils for us.

00:03:21.239 --> 00:03:26.989
The search for a way to make biofuels has
taken on many forms in many different places.

00:03:26.989 --> 00:03:32.040
In the 1970s, the United States Department
of Energy started the Aquatic Species Program

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:35.400
to explore the possibility of getting algae
to make fuels.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:41.810
Of course, the process is more involved than
just sitting back and letting algae make oil for us.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:44.849
One microbe is just one microbe.

00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:49.300
Their real power happens when they are grown
in large quantities.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:54.930
But algae do not come equipped with a manual
on how to best grow them, nor do they tell

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:59.569
you how to extract and refine the oil they
produce.

00:03:59.569 --> 00:04:03.639
Scientists have had to unravel and optimize
those steps themselves.

00:04:03.639 --> 00:04:08.230
Fortunately, the simplicity of algae makes
them well-suited for experimentation.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:12.879
But technology isn’t just the sum total
of the research that’s made it possible.

00:04:12.879 --> 00:04:17.539
It’s also the economics of how it fits into
the world.

00:04:17.539 --> 00:04:21.870
And algae have proven much more challenging
in that regard.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:30.060
In 2010, the estimated cost of a barrel of
algae-based fuel was between $300-$2600, while

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:35.270
petroleum was just $40-80.

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:39.509
Many of the challenges that create that discrepancy
are biological.

00:04:39.509 --> 00:04:44.009
Botryococcus, for example, grows too slowly
to make it work for us.

00:04:44.009 --> 00:04:48.340
And other species and techniques have had
their own obstacles.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:53.250
The Aquatic Species Program ended in the mid-1990s,
and there have been a number of other companies

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:58.280
that have tried and failed to bring algae-based
biofuels to the market.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:04.380
But as is the case with so much of science,
failure doesn’t end a story, it just becomes

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:06.419
a part of another tale.

00:05:06.419 --> 00:05:12.690
The past few decades have been marked by major
advances in the tools we use to study biology

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:15.199
at the molecular and genetic level.

00:05:15.199 --> 00:05:22.780
And those tools are still always improving,
providing new ways to identify algae strains,

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:29.050
decipher their genes, and engineer new pathways
to maximize biofuel production.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:34.800
And even if we never figure out algae-based
biofuels, we’re still living a world made

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.270
all the richer because of algae.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:41.650
Because here’s the thing about biology:
we’ve come a long way in how we understand

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:46.449
it, but we are still really in our infancy.

00:05:46.449 --> 00:05:52.600
We are finger-painting with biology, while
nature sketches and colors masterpieces that

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:56.860
will always be eons ahead of us.

00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:59.880
But that does not mean we won’t get better.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:05.169
In fact we have to get better, not just for
the sake of progress, but for the sake of

00:06:05.169 --> 00:06:11.319
our survival—to make things that won’t
just improve our lives in the immediate future,

00:06:11.319 --> 00:06:16.850
but will also leave our future selves without
catastrophic problems.

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:21.620
We may figure out biofuels one day, but it
will come with new questions and challenges

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:25.460
about how they are made and how we consume.

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:32.629
Human progress has always demonstrated both
our ingenuity and short-sightedness.

00:06:32.629 --> 00:06:38.250
And the more we understand the microcosmos,
the more we will seek to use it.

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:43.600
But to use it well, we have to understand
how it fits with the world we have today,

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:50.080
and the world that we want to exist tomorrow.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:56.169
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

